findings.claim:
Read-Jsonl parses every line with ConvertFrom-Json, and $ErrorActionPreference='Stop' is set. One unparseable line in marks.jsonl or arms.jsonl therefore aborts -Report:
- it exits 1 with a ConvertFrom-Json exception;
- it prints neither RUN COMPLETE nor RUN INCOMPLETE, no MISSING lines and no raw nets;
- that breaks the contract's 'exit 0 complete, 2 incomplete'.

A torn last line is what a kill or console close mid-Add-Content leaves. A NUL-filled tail is what a bugcheck leaves when file data is not flushed. A NUL tail also survives the `$_.Trim()` filter, because NUL is not whitespace. Both are the likely ending of a kernel-pool session, and that is when -Report is needed most.

I reproduced it with a run dir holding a plan line, a good R0 mark and the truncated line `{"kind":"mark","label":"I0/start","pagedBytes":1,"nonpa`. The output was: `ConvertFrom-Json: ... PoolWatch.ps1:382 ... Unterminated string`, exit=1.

Invoke-ExeArm starts FsAnalyser.exe and then polls the guard with Get-Pool (Get-CimInstance) and Write-Mark. Nothing protects that loop.

A terminating error, or Ctrl+C from the person, ends -Protocol while the exe is still running:
- the error can come from Get-Pool or from Get-Boot inside Write-Mark, because 'Stop' turns any CIM failure terminating;
- FsAnalyser.exe keeps scanning C: with no guard polling, so the promised 'kills FsAnalyser.exe' does not happen;
- the arm's line is never written to arms.jsonl.

-Report then lists the arm as 'in the plan but not in arms.jsonl, so it never started', although marks.jsonl holds its /before mark and its tNNN marks. On a single-use boot, that misstates what happened to the arm.

findings.fix:
Wrap :342-375 in try/finally. In the finally:
- if `-not $p.HasExited`, run `$p.Kill(); $p.WaitForExit()`;
- set `$line['interrupted'] = $true`;
- Add-Line the arm line.

At :397, when `$byLabel.ContainsKey("$name/before")`, say 'started (its /before mark is in marks.jsonl) but its arm line was never written' instead of 'never started'.

